# PRANSETU backend configuration — template.
#
# Copy this file to `backend/.env`, then fill in the real values:
#
#     cp backend/env.example backend/.env
#
# CORRECTION TO EARLIER INSTRUCTIONS: a previous version of this template also
# carried SUPABASE_DB_URL, and copying it verbatim made the API fail at startup —
#
#     ValidationError: 1 validation error for Settings
#     supabase_db_url  Extra inputs are not permitted [type=extra_forbidden]
#
# pydantic-settings applies extra='forbid' to keys read from a dotenv *file*
# (extras in the real process environment are ignored, which is why the same
# variable is fine as a shell export). The database URL has therefore been moved
# out of this file — see MIGRATIONS at the bottom. Copying this template now
# produces a .env that boots.
#
# None of the secrets below should ever be pasted into a chat, a screenshot, or a
# commit. `.gitignore` covers `.env` at any depth (verified).


# ══ Required — the API will not start without these ═══════════════════════════

# Project Settings → API → Project URL. Public; safe to expose.
SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co

# Project Settings → API → Project API keys → `service_role`.
#
# SERVER-ONLY SECRET, and the most dangerous value in this file: it bypasses
# every row-level security policy in the database. Never ship it to the browser
# and never put it in a VITE_* variable — anything prefixed VITE_ is compiled
# into the public JavaScript bundle.
SUPABASE_KEY=YOUR_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY

# Project Settings → API → Project API keys → `anon` (a.k.a. publishable).
#
# Needed as a *separate* client from the service_role one above. Password
# verification calls sign_in_with_password(), which mutates the session state of
# whichever client it is called on; doing that on the service_role client would
# leave a shared, RLS-bypassing client carrying a logged-in user's session.
# Safe to expose — it is RLS-constrained by design.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=YOUR_ANON_KEY

# Signing key for PRANSETU's own session tokens.
#
# Deliberately NOT the Supabase JWT secret. Supabase-issued tokens cannot be
# revoked before they expire, so "sign out", "sign out from all devices" and
# administrative session termination are all impossible with them. The backend
# therefore mints its own short-lived token carrying a session id, checked
# against public.user_sessions on every request — and signs it with this key.
#
# Generate a fresh one (do not reuse any other secret, and do not use the value
# shown as the placeholder):
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
#
# Rotating this key invalidates every active session immediately, which is the
# intended emergency lever.
PRANSETU_JWT_SECRET=GENERATE_A_RANDOM_64_BYTE_VALUE_SEE_COMMENT_ABOVE


# ══ Recommended ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Browser origins allowed to call this API, comma-separated, no trailing slash.
#
# This replaces allow_origins=["*"], which the browser silently refuses to
# combine with credentialed requests — and which, if it did work, would let any
# website on the internet issue authenticated calls with an operator's session.
#
# Add the deployed frontend origin before going live; localhost alone will block
# production traffic.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5190,http://127.0.0.1:5190


# ══ Optional ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Project Settings → API → JWT Settings → JWT Secret.
#
# LEGACY / usually unnecessary. Session tokens are signed with
# PRANSETU_JWT_SECRET (above), so the backend no longer needs this. Newer
# Supabase projects sign with ES256/RS256 and may not expose an HS256 secret at
# all — leave it blank in that case. Kept only for projects still holding one.
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# Only needed if you exercise the IVR / SMS webhook paths.
#
# While this sits at its placeholder value the Twilio signature check passes
# unconditionally, which means the IVR webhook accepts unsigned requests from
# anyone. Set a real token before exposing that endpoint publicly.
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=


# ══ MIGRATIONS — a shell variable, NOT a line in this file ════════════════════
#
# Applying supabase/migrations/*.sql needs the database connection string. It is
# kept out of .env on purpose: it embeds the database password, and the running
# API has no reason to hold that in memory. Export it only for the push, in the
# shell, so it never lands on disk:
#
#     export SUPABASE_DB_URL='postgresql://postgres.REF:ENCODED_PW@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres'
#     npx supabase db push --db-url "$SUPABASE_DB_URL"
#
# Project Settings → Database → Connection string → URI. Two things to get right:
#
#   * Choose "Session pooler" — port 5432 on aws-*.pooler.supabase.com. The
#     transaction pooler on 6543 cannot run DDL. The direct db.<ref>.supabase.co
#     host is IPv6-only on newer projects and typically will not resolve from a
#     home connection.
#
#   * PERCENT-ENCODE the password. Unencoded, a special character either fails to
#     parse or silently redirects the connection to a different host:
#         @ → %40    # → %23    ? → %3F    / → %2F    : → %3A    % → %25
